<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_create.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-create">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-create">
  <primary>CYPHER CREATE</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CYPHER CREATE</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CYPHER CREATE</refname>
  <refpurpose>create the cypher elements</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>

CREATE <literal>path_pattern</literal>

<literal>path_pattern</literal> can be one of:

    <literal>node_pattern</literal>
    <literal>path_pattern</literal> <literal>edge_pattern</literal> <literal>node_pattern</literal>
    <replaceable class="parameter">path_variable_name</replaceable> = <literal>path_pattern</literal>

and <literal>node_pattern</literal> is:

    ( [<replaceable class="parameter">variable_name</replaceable>][<replaceable class="parameter">:label_name</replaceable>] [ ONLY ] [ <literal>cypher_property_map</literal> ] )

and <literal>edge_pattern</literal> is:

    { - | &lt;- } [ [<replaceable class="parameter">variable_name</replaceable>][<replaceable class="parameter">:label_name</replaceable>] [ONLY] [ <literal>cypher_range_pattern</literal> ] [ <literal>cypher_property_map</literal> ] ] { - | -&gt; }

and <literal>cypher_property_map</literal> is:

    { <replaceable class="parameter">property_name</replaceable> : <replaceable class="parameter">property_value</replaceable>, [ ...] }

</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   The <command>CREATE</command> clause is a clause that creates graph elements such as vertices and edges.
When you want to create a vertex, you need to write a vertex-related pattern in the CREATE clause. 
You may create each element separately, but you may also create a pattern you want
at a time by listing the path in the CREATE clause. Be careful when creating a path at once. 
If it is not used with the MATCH clause like the first query, it creates a new data
even if there is the same data as the corresponding pattern (i.e. duplicate data).

You can use the <command>CREATE</command> command both standalone statement and clause that working with of another clause.
  </para>

 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <refsect2>
   <title><literal>path_pattern</literal></title>
   <para>
    Mainly, represent about the cypher path expression.
    See <xref linkend="cypher-path-pattern"/> for more examples.
   </para>
  </refsect2>

 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   To create vertex:
<programlisting>
CREATE ( );
CREATE (:person);
CREATE (:person {name: 'Edward'});
CREATE (:person {name: 'Alice', age: 20});
CREATE (a {name:'Alice'}), (b {name:a.name})
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   To create edge:
<programlisting>
MATCH (E:person {name: 'Edward'}), (A:person {name: 'Alice'})
CREATE (E)-[:likes]->(A);

MATCH (E:person {name: 'Edward'}), (A:person {name: 'Alice'})
CREATE (E)-[:likes {why: 'She is lovely'}]->(A);

MATCH (E:person {name: 'Edward'})
CREATE (E)-[:IS_PROUD_OF]->(E);
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   To create path:
<programlisting>
CREATE (E:person {name: 'Edward'})-[:likes]->(A:person {name: 'Alice'});

MATCH (E:person {name: 'Edward'})
CREATE (E)-[:likes]->(A:person {name: 'Alice'});

MATCH (E:person {name: 'Edward'}), (A:person {name: 'Alice'})
CREATE (E)-[:likes]->(A);
</programlisting>
  </para>

 </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
  <title>See Also</title>

  <simplelist type="inline">
   <member><xref linkend="cypher-match"/></member>
  </simplelist>
 </refsect1>
</refentry>



